"""Connect academic questions, practical development and aesthetic decisions."""
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

def add(root):
    path=root / 'labs/academic-library.html'
    s=BeautifulSoup(path.read_text(encoding='utf-8'),'html.parser')
    content='''<section class="al-reading" id="building-and-research"><p class="al-tag">MAKE SOMETHING / INVESTIGATE SOMETHING</p><h2>Building it and studying it can work together.</h2><p>A game, app or company website can be both a practical project and something you study. “Game studies” is a research field. “Game development” is a making practice. An app is a type of product. None of these tells us the whole genre or purpose.</p><div class="pa-routes"><section><h3>Game studies</h3><p><strong>Ask:</strong> how do rules, stories, players and communities make meaning?</p><p><strong>Try:</strong> closely analyse one playable moment or observe how players understand it.</p><p><strong>Search:</strong> <code>game studies player agency</code>, <code>game aesthetics</code>, <code>Kristine Jørgensen game interfaces</code>.</p><a href="https://gamestudies.org/about.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Game Studies: an academic journal</a></section><section><h3>Game development</h3><p><strong>Build:</strong> mechanics, levels, dialogue, feedback or a working prototype.</p><p><strong>Ask:</strong> does the design produce the experience you intended?</p><p><strong>Try:</strong> change one mechanic, playtest both versions and compare what players do.</p><p><strong>Search:</strong> <code>game design playtesting</code>, <code>level design player guidance</code>.</p><a href="#jorgensen-start">Connect your prototype to interface research</a></section><section><h3>App and web development</h3><p><strong>Build:</strong> an app, website, interface or useful tool.</p><p><strong>Ask:</strong> can the intended users complete the task, including people using assistive technology?</p><p><strong>Try:</strong> watch someone attempt a realistic task. Check keyboard access, feedback and errors.</p><p><strong>Search:</strong> <code>human computer interaction usability</code>, <code>accessible interaction design</code>.</p><a href="accessibility-lab.html">Try the accessibility workshop</a></section><section><h3>Interactive work for a company or organisation</h3><p><strong>Build:</strong> a visitor experience, event guide, training tool, exhibition or digital service.</p><p><strong>Ask:</strong> whose problem are you solving, and does the solution work for those people?</p><p><strong>Try:</strong> turn the brief into a testable task. Compare what the organisation wants with what participants need.</p><p><strong>Search:</strong> <code>participatory design</code>, <code>service design user research</code>, <code>design justice</code>.</p><a href="#across-fields">See how Ingrid combined work and study</a></section></div><p>You can combine these routes. For example: build a game for an organisation, study its interface and investigate inclusion. Follow your assignment requirements when deciding what to document.</p></section>
<section class="al-reading" id="aesthetics"><p class="al-tag">AESTHETICS LAB / HOW DOES IT FEEL, AND WHY?</p><h2>Aesthetics is part of how the work communicates.</h2><p>Colour, shape, type, images, sound, movement, pace and space can make an experience feel welcoming, tense, playful, confusing or calm. Aesthetics also asks how people experience and judge a work. It is more than adding decoration.</p><div class="ae-try"><h3>Same task. Different mood.</h3><p>Switch the style. The words and destination stay the same. Notice what the visual treatment suggests.</p><div class="ae-switches" role="group" aria-label="Choose a visual style"><button type="button" data-ae-style="calm" aria-pressed="true">Calm</button><button type="button" data-ae-style="arcade" aria-pressed="false">Arcade</button><button type="button" data-ae-style="editorial" aria-pressed="false">Editorial</button></div><div class="ae-demo" data-ae-preview="calm"><p class="ae-eyebrow">PRIDE PARK / VISITOR GUIDE</p><h4>Find your place.</h4><p>Explore the park and discover the people who make it feel like home.</p><a href="lab-programme.html#mapping-first-contact">Open the map workshop →</a></div><p id="ae-status" role="status">Calm: quiet colours, generous space and a simple type style.</p><p>This is a teaching example, not a screenshot of Ingrid's Pride Park game. These are possible design effects, not findings about what every person feels.</p></div><div class="pa-routes"><section><h3>Look and listen</h3><p>What do colour, contrast, type, images, sound and timing suggest? Do the parts support each other?</p><p>Search <code>visual rhetoric</code>, <code>sound aesthetics</code> or <code>game feel</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Connect to meaning</h3><p>Why is this treatment appropriate for this story, audience or organisation? “I like it” can start the discussion, but explain what the choice does.</p><p>Search <code>aesthetic experience</code>, <code>phenomenology</code> or <code>affect digital media</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Test the experience</h3><p>Make two versions of one screen or scene. Keep the task the same. Ask someone what the style suggests, then watch whether they can use it.</p><p>Separate “it looks welcoming” from “I can find the next step”. Appearance and usability can interact, but they are not the same.</p></section><section><h3>Include different people</h3><p>Check readable text, keyboard focus, captions where needed and reduced-motion preferences. Never use colour alone to carry essential information.</p><a href="accessibility-lab.html">Connect aesthetics to accessibility →</a></section></div><p><a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aesthetic-judgment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Aesthetic Judgment</a> · <a href="https://research.google/blog/is-beautiful-usable-what-is-the-influence-of-beauty-and-usability-on-reactions-to-a-product/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Research: appearance and usability as distinct factors</a></p><p><strong>Take it into your project:</strong> change one aesthetic choice, keep the interaction the same and compare the experience.</p><a href="#sources">Next: choose a source for your question →</a></section>'''
    nodes=BeautifulSoup(content,'html.parser')
    for node in list(nodes.contents): s.select_one('#across-fields').insert_before(node)
    nav=s.select_one('.al-hero nav')
    for href,label in [('#building-and-research','Making and research'),('#aesthetics','Aesthetics lab')]:
        link=s.new_tag('a',href=href);link.string=label;nav.append(link)
    result = s.select_one('#rf-result')
    result.append(BeautifulSoup('<p><a href="#aesthetics">Explore how your project looks, sounds and feels →</a></p>','html.parser').find())
    s.head.append(BeautifulSoup('<link rel="stylesheet" href="library-workshop.css?v=1"/><script defer src="library-workshop.js?v=1"></script>','html.parser'))
    path.write_text(str(s),encoding='utf-8',newline='\n')
